---
/**
 * Politique de signature du code : où en est la signature des installeurs, comment ils sont construits, qui publie, la
 * confidentialité, et comment vérifier un fichier (empreinte SHA-256, rapport VirusTotal). Liée depuis le pied de page
 * et la page de téléchargement. `SIGNED_BUILDS.windows` passe à vrai quand l'installeur Windows est signé.
 */
import LegalDoc from "@/layouts/LegalDoc.astro";
import { LINKS } from "@/lib/site";
import { SIGNED_BUILDS } from "@/lib/release";

const WORKFLOW = `${LINKS.github}/blob/main/.github/workflows/desktop-release.yml`;
---

<LegalDoc
  title="Code signing policy"
  description="How TaskForce Desktop is built and published, whether its installers are signed, and how to check a download."
  updated="20 October 2026"
>
  <h2>Signing</h2>
  {
    SIGNED_BUILDS.windows ? (
      <p>The TaskForce Desktop installer for Windows is code-signed.</p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        The installers of TaskForce Desktop are not code-signed yet. Windows, and some antivirus programs that do not
        know a new file yet, may warn you before running it: the <a href="/download#install">download page</a> says what
        to do. Until the installers are signed, the sections below say how each file is built and how to check it.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2>How a release is built</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The installers (Windows <code>.exe</code>, Linux <code>.deb</code> and AppImage) are published on the{" "}
      <a href="/download">download page</a> and in the project's GitHub releases.
    </li>
    <li>
      They are built from the public source code of the <code>main</code> branch by the{" "}
      <a href={WORKFLOW}>Desktop - Release</a> workflow on GitHub Actions. Nothing built anywhere else is published.
    </li>
    <li>
      The app bundles open source components from other projects (Electron, a Java runtime, PostgreSQL). They keep
      their own publishers' signatures, or none.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Team and roles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Committers and reviewers:</strong> Pierre Michel (<a href="https://github.com/Miche1-Pierre">Miche1-Pierre</a>).
      Changes from anyone outside the team come as pull requests, reviewed by a committer before they are merged.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Release approvers:</strong> Pierre Michel. Every release is started by hand.
    </li>
    <li>Team members must use two-factor authentication on GitHub.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>
    What TaskForce Desktop sends, and to whom, is described in our <a href="/legal/privacy">privacy policy</a>: it signs
    you in to your TaskForce account, asks GitHub whether a new version exists, and sends data to the AI providers and
    services you choose to connect. It sends us no usage data.
  </p>

  <h2>Check a download</h2>
  <p>
    Each file of a release shows its SHA-256 digest on the <a href="/download">download page</a> and on its{" "}
    <a href={`${LINKS.github}/releases`}>GitHub release page</a>. On Windows, compare it with{" "}
    <code>Get-FileHash TaskForce-Setup-&lt;version&gt;.exe</code> in PowerShell; on Linux, with{" "}
    <code>sha256sum</code>. When a file has been sent to VirusTotal, its report is linked next to it on the download
    page.
  </p>
</LegalDoc>
